WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.502
JavaScript applications process untrusted data constantly.

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:07.306
A browser application receives form fields, query strings, storage values, messages, files,

00:00:07.306 --> 00:00:09.307
and data from third-party services.

00:00:09.307 --> 00:00:10.108
A Node.

00:00:10.108 --> 00:00:14.511
js service receives request bodies, route parameters, headers, cookies, WebSocket messages,

00:00:14.511 --> 00:00:17.313
environment variables, queue events, and API responses.

00:00:17.313 --> 00:00:22.117
Input validation is the defensive boundary that asks whether a value is

00:00:22.117 --> 00:00:26.921
acceptable before the application trusts it or uses it to make decisions.

00:00:26.921 --> 00:00:30.924
JavaScript's flexibility is productive, but it also creates validation risk.

00:00:30.924 --> 00:00:35.728
Dynamic typing and type coercion can make values behave in unexpected ways

00:00:35.728 --> 00:00:38.130
if checks are loose or ambiguous.

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:43.734
A string may be compared like a number, an empty value may become false,

00:00:43.734 --> 00:00:47.737
an array may be accepted where an object was expected,

00:00:47.737 --> 00:00:52.941
and a missing field may move through code until it causes unsafe behavior.

00:00:52.941 --> 00:00:55.744
Clear runtime validation makes those assumptions explicit.

00:00:55.744 --> 00:01:00.948
Validation is related to other controls, but it is not the same thing.

00:01:00.948 --> 00:01:03.750
Parsing turns raw input into structured data.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:06.552
Normalization converts data into a consistent representation.

00:01:06.552 --> 00:01:10.555
Sanitization changes data, often to remove or transform unwanted parts.

00:01:10.555 --> 00:01:16.160
Output encoding prepares data for a specific output context such as HTML, JavaScript, URLs,

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.761
logs, or SQL parameters.

00:01:17.761 --> 00:01:22.164
Validation decides whether the application should accept the value at all.

00:01:22.164 --> 00:01:25.767
Input validation is one layer of a secure design.

00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:30.571
It does not replace authorization, output encoding, parameterized queries, safe file handling,

00:01:30.571 --> 00:01:33.373
safe command execution, or business logic checks.

00:01:33.373 --> 00:01:34.974
Its value is predictability.

00:01:34.974 --> 00:01:38.577
When JavaScript applications validate inputs at the right boundaries,

00:01:38.577 --> 00:01:42.180
they reduce surprises before untrusted data reaches sensitive code.
